<BaseLayout title="Methodology — Freighthub" description="How Freighthub researches, labels and updates China–GCC freight intelligence, including how we separate reported facts, industry claims and editorial judgement.">
  <article>
    <h1>Methodology</h1>
    <p class="article-meta">Editorial standards · Last reviewed 11 November 2026</p>

    <p>This page explains how Freighthub is researched, written and corrected. It is deliberately specific: a reader should be able to tell what kind of claim they are reading and how much weight to give it.</p>

    <h2>1. The question comes first</h2>
    <p>Every page starts with the decision a shipper actually faces: which port, which mode, who clears, what certificate, how much, how long, what can go wrong. The page is organised around that decision, not around a template. That is why the H2 order differs between countries and ports.</p>

    <h2>2. Three kinds of statement</h2>
    <p>We separate:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Reported</strong> — a fact from an official source, a port authority, a regulator or the trade press, cited at the bottom of the page.</li>
      <li><strong>Industry claim</strong> — a range, practice or expectation attributed to forwarders, carriers or importers, labelled as a claim rather than a verified fact.</li>
      <li><strong>Editorial judgement</strong> — our assessment of what the evidence means. It is labelled, arguable and never presented as a regulator's position.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The labels are not decoration. They tell the reader which statements can be checked against a document and which are an opinion.</p>

    <h2>3. Source hierarchy</h2>
    <p>When sources conflict, we prefer:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Primary official sources: customs authorities, standards bodies, port authorities, tax authorities, the IMO and IATA.</li>
      <li>Carrier and terminal operator publications.</li>
      <li>Recognised trade press and freight indices.</li>
      <li>Forwarder and industry commentary, treated as a claim.</li>
      <li>Chinese-language trade sources, used for operational texture and cross-checked where possible.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>A single source is rarely enough for a number that a reader might budget against. Where a figure is uncertain, we give a range and say what moves it.</p>

    <h2>4. What we will not publish</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Precise freight rates presented as current prices.</li>
      <li>Guaranteed transit times.</li>
      <li>Client names or case studies that identify a company without consent.</li>
      <li>Certifications, awards or credentials we do not hold.</li>
      <li>Invented sources, dates or quotations.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Where a page uses a range, the range is a planning aid. It is not a quotation and should not be treated as one.</p>

    <h2>5. How the pages are built</h2>
    <p>Each major page normally contains:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>A direct answer in the first 100 words.</li>
      <li>A key-facts table with the numbers that matter.</li>
      <li>An explanation of what changed or what is structurally different.</li>
      <li>Cost and transit drivers, with ranges rather than point prices.</li>
      <li>Risk and compliance failure modes.</li>
      <li>An editorial judgement, clearly labelled.</li>
      <li>An FAQ that answers the questions a buyer would actually ask.</li>
      <li>Sources and an update log.</li>
      <li>Internal links to the related country, port, method and sector pages.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Not every page uses every module. A comparison page leads with a verdict; a port page leads with the terminal and landside reality. The structure follows the decision.</p>

    <h2>6. AI assistance</h2>
    <p>Freighthub uses AI tools for research support, drafting and editing. The editorial desk sets the questions, checks the sources, edits the copy and owns the judgements. AI is not credited as an author, and it is not used to invent facts or sources. When a claim cannot be checked, it is labelled as a claim or removed.</p>

    <h2>7. Dates and updates</h2>
    <p>Every page carries a <em>last reviewed</em> date. A review means the page has been checked for factual accuracy, broken links and stale ranges. It does not mean every sentence has changed.</p>
    <p>The <a href="/updates/">updates feed</a> carries dated briefings. Each entry states what changed, why it matters, who is affected and what to do. The feed is not a news wire; it is a set of operational notes.</p>

    <h2>8. Corrections</h2>
    <p>Errors are corrected on the page and logged in <a href="/corrections/">corrections</a>. A correction is a factual fix; a clarification is a wording fix; an update is new information. We do not silently delete pages to avoid a correction.</p>

    <h2>9. What this site is not</h2>
    <p>Freighthub is not a forwarder, a broker, a carrier or a marketplace. It does not sell freight, take bookings or earn commissions on shipments. It is an editorial project. The advice is general and should be checked against the current regulations and the specific shipment.</p>

    <h2>10. Contact</h2>
    <p>Questions, corrections and source suggestions: <a href="/contact/">contact the editorial desk</a>.</p>
